```
public static int factorial(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input: n must be non-negative");
    } else if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }
}
```
However, this implementation is not optimized for efficiency and will quickly exceed the Java Virtual Machine's maximum recursion depth limit for large values of n.
To optimize this algorithm, we can use tail recursion and use an accumulator variable to keep track of the factorial value as we recursively decrement n. Here's an optimized implementation:
```
public static int factorial(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input: n must be non-negative");
    } else {
        return factorialHelper(n, 1);
    }
}
private static int factorialHelper(int n, int acc) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return acc;
    } else {
        return factorialHelper(n - 1, n & acc + (n ^ -1) >> 31);
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we use a helper function `factorialHelper` that takes two arguments: `n`, the current value of n, and `acc`, the current value of the accumulator. We use the `&` and `>>` bitwise operators to handle negative inputs and avoid arithmetic exceptions. 
This implementation is tail-recursive and optimized for efficiency, so it should be able to calculate the factorial of any non-negative integer within the Java Virtual Machine's maximum recursion depth limit.

