To analyze the data in the table and propose solutions to reduce the environmental impact of technology, we can use various algorithms and methods, such as data mining, machine learning, and statistical analysis. Here is an example of how we can use Java programming to perform a statistical analysis of the data and identify areas where improvements can be made:
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
public class EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer {

    private List<Device> devices;

    public EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer(List<Device> devices) {
        this.devices = devices;
    }

    public void analyze() {
        // Calculate average carbon footprint, water usage, and energy consumption for each type of device
        double laptopCarbonFootprint = calculateAverageCarbonFootprint(DeviceType.LAPTOP);
        double laptopWaterUsage = calculateAverageWaterUsage(DeviceType.LAPTOP);
        double laptopEnergyConsumption = calculateAverageEnergyConsumption(DeviceType.LAPTOP);

        double smartphoneCarbonFootprint = calculateAverageCarbonFootprint(DeviceType.SMARTPHONE);
        double smartphoneWaterUsage = calculateAverageWaterUsage(DeviceType.SMARTPHONE);
        double smartphoneEnergyConsumption = calculateAverageEnergyConsumption(DeviceType.SMARTPHONE);

        double serverCarbonFootprint = calculateAverageCarbonFootprint(DeviceType.SERVER);
        double serverWaterUsage = calculateAverageWaterUsage(DeviceType.SERVER);
        double serverEnergyConsumption = calculateAverageEnergyConsumption(DeviceType.SERVER);

        // Identify areas where improvements can be made
        if (laptopCarbonFootprint > smartphoneCarbonFootprint) {
            System.out.println("Laptops have a higher carbon footprint than smartphones.");
            System.out.println("To reduce the environmental impact of laptops, we can encourage manufacturers to use more sustainable materials and energy-efficient components.");
        }

        if (serverEnergyConsumption > laptopEnergyConsumption && serverEnergyConsumption > smartphoneEnergyConsumption) {
            System.out.println("Servers have a higher energy consumption than laptops and smartphones.");
            System.out.println("To reduce the environmental impact of servers, we can optimize data center cooling systems and increase the use of renewable energy sources.");
        }

        if (laptopWaterUsage > smartphoneWaterUsage) {
            System.out.println("Laptops have a higher water usage than smartphones.");
            System.out.println("To reduce the environmental impact of laptops, we can encourage manufacturers to use water-efficient manufacturing processes and reduce the use of water-intensive materials.");
        }
    }

    private double calculateAverageCarbonFootprint(DeviceType deviceType) {
        List<Device> devicesOfType = devices.stream()
                                            .filter(device -> device.getDeviceType() == deviceType)
                                            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        double totalCarbonFootprint = devicesOfType.stream()
                                                   .mapToDouble(Device::getCarbonFootprint)
                                                   .sum();
        return totalCarbonFootprint / devicesOfType.size();
    }

    private double calculateAverageWaterUsage(DeviceType deviceType) {
        List<Device> devicesOfType = devices.stream()
                                            .filter(device -> device.getDeviceType() == deviceType)
                                            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        double totalWaterUsage = devicesOfType.stream()
                                              .mapToDouble(Device::getWaterUsage)
                                              .sum();
        return totalWaterUsage / devicesOfType.size();
    }

    private double calculateAverageEnergyConsumption(DeviceType deviceType) {
        List<Device> devicesOfType = devices.stream()
                                            .filter(device -> device.getDeviceType() == deviceType)
                                            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        double totalEnergyConsumption = devicesOfType.stream()
                                                     .mapToDouble(Device::getEnergyConsumption)
                                                     .sum();
        return totalEnergyConsumption / devicesOfType.size();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Device> devices = new ArrayList<>();
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.LAPTOP, 2.5, 100, 50));
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.LAPTOP, 3.0, 120, 60));
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.SMARTPHONE, 1.0, 20, 10));
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.SMARTPHONE, 1.5, 30, 15));
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.SERVER, 10.0, 500, 1000));
        devices.add(new Device(DeviceType.SERVER, 12.0, 600, 1200));

        EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer analyzer = new EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer(devices);
        analyzer.analyze();
    }
}
class Device {
    private DeviceType deviceType;
    private double carbonFootprint;
    private double waterUsage;
    private double energyConsumption;

    public Device(DeviceType deviceType, double carbonFootprint, double waterUsage, double energyConsumption) {
        this.deviceType = deviceType;
        this.carbonFootprint = carbonFootprint;
        this.waterUsage = waterUsage;
        this.energyConsumption = energyConsumption;
    }

    public DeviceType getDeviceType() {
        return deviceType;
    }

    public double getCarbonFootprint() {
        return carbonFootprint;
    }

    public double getWaterUsage() {
        return waterUsage;
    }

    public double getEnergyConsumption() {
        return energyConsumption;
    }
}
enum DeviceType {
    LAPTOP,
    SMARTPHONE,
    SERVER
}
```
In this example, we define a `Device` class to represent each device in the table, and a `DeviceType` enum to specify the type of device. We also define an `EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer` class that takes a list of `Device` objects as input and performs a statistical analysis of the data.
The `analyze()` method calculates the average carbon footprint, water usage, and energy consumption for each type of device, and then identifies areas where improvements can be made based on the analysis. For example, if laptops have a higher carbon footprint than smartphones, the method suggests encouraging manufacturers to use more sustainable materials and energy-efficient components.
The `calculateAverageCarbonFootprint()`, `calculateAverageWaterUsage()`, and `calculateAverageEnergyConsumption()` methods use Java 8 streams to filter the list of devices by type and calculate the average value of the specified attribute.
To test the implementation, we create a list of `Device` objects and pass it to an instance of the `EnvironmentalImpactAnalyzer` class. The output of the program is:
