To remove all duplicate strings from an input array of strings in Go, we can use a map to keep track of the unique elements. Here is the Go code that implements this function:
```
package main
import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
)
func removeDuplicates(inputArray []string) []string {
    uniqueMap := make(map[string]bool)
    uniqueArray := []string{}
    for _, element := range inputArray {
        if _, ok := uniqueMap[element]; !ok {
            uniqueMap[element] = true
            uniqueArray = append(uniqueArray, element)
        }
    }
    return uniqueArray
}
func main() {
    input := []string{"apple", "banana", "orange", "apple", "grape", "banana", "watermelon", "grape"}
    output := removeDuplicates(input)
    jsonOutput, _ := json.Marshal(map[string][]string{"outputArray": output})
    fmt.Println(string(jsonOutput))
}
```
The `removeDuplicates` function takes an input array of strings and returns a new array with all duplicates removed. It uses a map to keep track of the unique elements. For each element in the input array, it checks if the element is already in the map. If it is not, it adds the element to the map and appends it to the output array.
