Calculating the median age at death for all genders and races in the US, taking into account socio-economic status, access to healthcare, and lifestyle choices would require a large and complex dataset that accounts for all of these factors. Unfortunately, I do not have access to such a dataset. However, I can provide a sample Swift code that demonstrates the calculation process for finding the median age of an array of numbers:
```
let ages = [23, 45, 67, 32, 56, 78, 43, 29, 61, 51, 37, 49, 54, 62, 28, 39, 41, 47, 57, 31]
let sortedAges = ages.sorted()
let count = ages.count
if count % 2 == 0 {
    let middle = count / 2
    let median = Double(sortedAges[middle] + sortedAges[middle - 1]) / 2
    print("The median age is \(median)")
} else {
    let middle = count / 2
    let median = Double(sortedAges[middle])
    print("The median age is \(median)")
}
```
This code creates an array of ages, sorts them in ascending order, and then calculates the median age by checking if the number of ages in the array is even or odd. If it is even, the median is the average of the two middle numbers. If it is odd, the median is the middle number. 
