Yes, I can help you with that. Here's the code for implementing Zeller's congruence formula in C++:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
string getDayOfWeek(int day, int month, int year)
{
    string daysOfWeek[] = {"Saturday", "Sunday", "Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday"};
    int q = day;
    int m = month;
    int y = year;
    if (m < 3)
    {
        m += 12;
        y--;
    }
    int k = y % 100;
    int j = y / 100;
    int h = (q + ((13 * (m + 1)) / 5) + k + (k / 4) + (j / 4) + (5 * j)) % 7;
    return daysOfWeek[h];
}
int main()
{
    int day, month, year;
    cout << "Enter date in MM/DD/YYYY format: ";
    scanf("%d/%d/%d", &month, &day, &year);
    string dayOfWeek = getDayOfWeek(day, month, year);
    cout << "The day of the week is: " << dayOfWeek << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define an array `daysOfWeek` that holds the names of the days of the week. We then define the `getDayOfWeek` function that takes in the day, month, and year as parameters and returns the corresponding day of the week using Zeller's congruence formula.
In the `main` function, we prompt the user to input the date in the format MM/DD/YYYY using `scanf`. We then call the `getDayOfWeek` function to get the corresponding day of the week and output it to the user.
Note that Zeller's congruence formula assumes the Gregorian calendar was adopted on October 15, 1582. If you need to handle dates before that, you will need to adjust the formula accordingly.

Sure, here is a code snippet that analyzes the list of names and identifies the ones that are not real people, as well as the common thread that connects the real names together:
```
import java.util.regex.*;
public class NameAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] names = {"Julie Jones", "Victor Johnson", "Terrence Smith", "Michelangelo Pickle", "Shana Washington"};
        String pattern = "^[A-Z][a-z]+\\s[A-Z][a-z]+$"; // regex pattern for real names

        for (String name : names) {
            if (!Pattern.matches(pattern, name)) {
                System.out.println(name + " is not a real name.");
            }
        }

        Pattern realNamePattern = Pattern.compile(pattern);
        Matcher matcher = realNamePattern.matcher(names[0]); // use first name as reference

        if (matcher.find()) {
            String commonPattern = matcher.group(); // get the common pattern from first name

            for (int i = 1; i < names.length; i++) {
                matcher.reset(names[i]);
                if (matcher.find() && !matcher.group().equals(commonPattern)) {
                    System.out.println("The common thread connecting real names is not consistent.");
                    break;
                }
            }

            System.out.println("The common thread connecting real names is: " + commonPattern);
        }
    }
}
```
The program first defines a regular expression pattern for real names, which matches names that start with a capital letter, followed by one or more lowercase letters, followed by a space, followed by another capital letter and one or more lowercase letters.
Next, the program loops through the list of names and checks if each name matches the real name pattern. If a name does not match the pattern, it is identified as not a real name.
Then, the program uses the first name in the list as a reference to identify the common thread connecting real names. It creates a regular expression matcher using the real name pattern and the first name, and finds the first occurrence of the pattern in the first name. It then loops through the remaining names and checks if they also match the pattern and have the same common thread as the first name. If all names match the pattern and have the same common thread, the program outputs the common thread. If not, the program outputs a message indicating that the common thread is not consistent.

Here is the C# code to generate a random alphanumeric string of length N that includes both upper and lowercase letters and digits:
```
public static string GenerateRandomString(int length)
{
    const string chars = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789";
    var random = new Random();
    return new string(Enumerable.Repeat(chars, length)
        .Select(s => s[random.Next(s.Length)]).ToArray());
}
```
This code uses the `Random` class to generate a random string of length `N` that includes upper and lowercase letters and digits. The `Enumerable.Repeat` method is used to repeat the `chars` string `N` times, and then the `Select` method is used to randomly select a character from each repeated string. Finally, the `new string` constructor is used to convert the resulting array of characters into a string.
